From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fanha Giang <fanha99@hotmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 09:16:48 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: open the conduit of a PHY-to-PHY CPU link

On IPQ5018 boards that wire the SoC GE-PHY straight into PHY4 of a QCA8337
(pz-l8, Redmi AX5400, Xiaomi CR8806, ...), the CPU port of the switch is
described with a "phy-handle" rather than a "fixed-link": the two ends of the
link are two PHYs, each owned by a different driver.

DSA registers and starts the switch-side PHY in dsa_port_setup() at probe
time, but the conduit's PHY belongs to the conduit netdev. stmmac, for
instance, attaches it in stmmac_init_phy(), which only runs from
stmmac_open(). Until something opens the conduit, the switch-side PHY
autonegotiates against a partner that advertises nothing, and the QCA8337
PHY4 clears its own 1000BASE-T advertisement about 13 s later; the link then
never comes up at gigabit, even once the conduit is opened.

Open the conduit before registering phylink for such a port, so that both
PHYs are up for the first autonegotiation.

A conduit that is itself a DSA user port - switches stacked onto one another -
is left alone: it is brought up through its own switch, and no such board has
been tested with this.

Signed-off-by: Fanha Giang <fanha99@hotmail.com>
---
--- a/net/dsa/port.c
+++ b/net/dsa/port.c
@@ -1881,6 +1881,46 @@ static void dsa_shared_port_link_down(st
 						   PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_NA);
 }
 
+/* A CPU or DSA port whose link is described by a "phy-handle" instead of a
+ * "fixed-link" is wired PHY to PHY: the PHY on the switch side negotiates
+ * against the PHY of the conduit. phylink starts the switch-side PHY here, at
+ * probe time, while the conduit's own PHY is attached only when the conduit is
+ * opened, which may be seconds later or never. The switch-side PHY then
+ * negotiates against a partner that advertises nothing, and some PHYs give up
+ * gigabit for good when that lasts too long (the QCA8337 PHY4 clears its
+ * 1000BASE-T advertisement after ~13 s). Open the conduit so that both ends of
+ * the link are brought up together.
+ *
+ * A conduit that is itself a DSA user port (switches stacked onto one another)
+ * is left alone: it is brought up through its own switch, and no such board
+ * has been tested with this.
+ */
+static void dsa_shared_port_open_conduit(struct dsa_port *dp)
+{
+	struct device_node *phy_np;
+	int err;
+
+	if (!dp->conduit || dsa_user_dev_check(dp->conduit) ||
+	    of_phy_is_fixed_link(dp->dn))
+		return;
+
+	phy_np = of_parse_phandle(dp->dn, "phy-handle", 0);
+	if (!phy_np)
+		return;
+	of_node_put(phy_np);
+
+	rtnl_lock();
+	err = dev_open(dp->conduit, NULL);
+	rtnl_unlock();
+
+	if (err)
+		dev_warn(dp->ds->dev,
+			 "failed to open conduit %s of %s port %d: %pe\n",
+			 dp->conduit->name,
+			 dsa_port_is_cpu(dp) ? "CPU" : "DSA", dp->index,
+			 ERR_PTR(err));
+}
+
 int dsa_shared_port_link_register_of(struct dsa_port *dp)
 {
 	struct dsa_switch *ds = dp->ds;
@@ -1902,6 +1942,8 @@ int dsa_shared_port_link_register_of(str
 	} else {
 		dsa_shared_port_link_down(dp);
 
+		dsa_shared_port_open_conduit(dp);
+
 		return dsa_shared_port_phylink_register(dp);
 	}
 
